window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["21-908"]={"d":"21-908","id":"2022/21-908","n":"Bartenwerfer v. Buckley","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioner Kate Bartenwerfer","date":"2022-07-19","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21-908/230392/20220719144833977_Bartenwerfer%20Opening%20Brief.pdf","side":"pet","n":49},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent Kieran Buckley","date":"2022-09-22","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21-908/238543/20220922123540131_21-908%20Buckley%20Br..pdf","side":"res","n":91},{"k":"united-states","label":"Brief amicus curiae of United States","date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21-908/242080/20220929135654610_21-908bsacUnitedStates.pdf","side":"us","n":69},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioner Kate Bartenwerfer","date":"2022-10-24","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21-908/243749/20221024131709229_Bartenwerfer%20Reply%20Brief.pdf","side":"pet","n":40}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/22pdf/598us1r5_1b72.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Alexander v. Sandoval","cite":"532 U.S. 275","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1908","sc":1,"by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Atherton v. FDIC","cite":"519 U.S. 213","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-928","sc":1,"by":{"0":"43","3":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bank of Am. Nat’l Tr. & Sav. Ass’n v. 203 N. LaSalle St. P’ship","cite":"526 U.S. 434","y":1999,"id":"1998/97-1418","sc":1,"by":{"0":"44"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"makes little sense to graft onto the Code concepts that were developed during a quite different era.","cite":"526 U.S. 434, 461-62"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Bostock v. Clayton County","cite":"140 S. Ct. 1731","y":2020,"id":"2019/17-1618","sc":1,"by":{"0":"18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[a] particular being as distinguished from a class, species, or collection.","cite":"140 S. Ct. 1731, 1741"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Briggs v. Spaulding","cite":"141 U.S. 132","y":1891,"id":"scdb/1890-297","sc":1,"by":{"0":"41, 43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bullock v. BankChampaign, N.A.","cite":"569 U.S. 267","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-1518","sc":1,"by":{"0":"13, 17, 29, 47","1":"32","2":"14","3":"3, 12, 18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"strong, special policy considerations, such as the presence of fault,","cite":"569 U.S. at 276"},{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[E]xceptions to discharge ‘should be confined to those plainly expressed.’","cite":"569 U.S. at 275"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"[e]xceptions to discharge ‘should be confined to those plainly expressed,’","cite":"569 U.S. 267, 275"},{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"strong, special policy considerations, such as the presence of fault.","cite":"569 U.S. at 276"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"And Bullock confirms that because, in that case, the Court was confronting whether defalcation requires intent, and the Court said that the (a)(4) exception for defalcation, along with the (a)(2)(A) exception for fraud, the (a)(6) exception for willful and malicious injury to property, I could go on, there's a couple of other tort-related things --"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"And that's evident from cases like Bullock, where defalcation had a very different meaning under state law, it had no intent requirement, and the Court said, no, bankruptcy discharge is a federal law."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"So I think the -- the justification why we're not reading something into the text is, under that line of logic, this Court's decision in Bullock probably should have come out the other way, because you start from the premise in bankruptcy, of course, there's a state law liability."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Cent. Bank of Denver, N.A. v. First Interstate Bank of Denver, N.A.","cite":"511 U.S. 164","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-854","sc":1,"by":{"0":"43","3":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cent. Va. Cmty. Coll. v. Katz","cite":"546 U.S. 356","y":2006,"id":"2005/04-885","sc":1,"by":{"0":"16"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"as much concerned with ensuring full satisfaction of creditors … as with securing new beginnings for debtors.","cite":"546 U.S. 356, 365"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Cohen v. de la Cruz","cite":"523 U.S. 213","y":1998,"id":"1997/96-1923","sc":1,"by":{"0":"18, 20, 28","1":"passim","2":"passim","3":"passim"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"most straightforward reading of § 523(a)(2)(A) is that it prevents the discharge of ‘any debt’ respecting ‘money, property, services, or … credit’ that the debtor has fraudulently obtained.","cite":"523 U.S. 213, 218"},{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"prohibit[ing] debtors from discharging liabilities incurred on account of their fraud, embodying a basic policy animating the Code of affording relief only to an ‘honest but unfortunate debtor.’","cite":"523 U.S. at 217"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"‘any debt’ arising therefrom is excepted from discharge.","cite":"523 U.S. 213, 218"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"modifies ‘money, property, services, or ... credit[,]’ … mak[ing] clear that the share of money, property, etc., that is obtained by fraud gives rise to a nondischargeable debt.","cite":"523 U.S. at 218"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"prevents the discharge of all liability arising from fraud.","cite":"523 U.S. 213, 215"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"A ‘debt’ is defined in the Code as ‘liability on a claim,’ a ‘claim’ is defined in turn as a ‘right to payment,’ and a ‘right to payment,’ [the Court has] said, ‘is nothing more nor less than an enforceable obligation’","cite":"523 U.S. 213, 218"},{"src":"argument","who":"Mr. Tripp","text":"It says that the -- the bankruptcy discharge \"does not discharge an individual debtor from any debt [...] for money [...] to the extent obtained [...] by actual fraud.\" So, as this Court put it in Cohen versus de la Cruz, once it is established that specific money is obtained by fraud, then \"any debt arising therefrom is not\" discharged."},{"src":"argument","who":"Mr. Tripp","text":"So I -- I think there are basically three pieces to the -- to the statutory test that this Court stated in Cohen. There is the liability piece, right, you need to owe a debt, which is defined to mean a liability that's usually going to come from state law."},{"src":"argument","who":"Mr. Tripp","text":"I think, really, the -- the better reading of the text as a whole is simply actually what the Court already said when it was paraphrasing the language of the -- of the test in Cohen versus de la Cruz."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Crawford v. Burke","cite":"195 U.S. 176","y":1904,"id":"scdb/1904-044","sc":1,"by":{"0":"37, 45"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[W]hether the discharge of the defendants in bankruptcy shall operate as a discharge of plaintiff’s debt, it not having been reduced to judgment, depends upon … whether it was or was not created by defendant’s fraud,","cite":"195 U.S. at 186"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Doyle v. First Nat’l Bank of Balt.","cite":"231 F. 649","y":1916,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./231/649/","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"E.I. du Pont de Nemours & Co. v. Train","cite":"430 U.S. 112","y":1977,"id":"1976/75-978","sc":1,"by":{"0":"20, 31","2":"25"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"does not itself answer this question.","cite":"430 U.S. 112, 128"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Erie R.R. Co. v. Tompkins","cite":"304 U.S. 64","y":1938,"id":"1900-1940/304us64","sc":1,"by":{"0":"passim","2":"21","3":"2, 22, 24, 25"},"q":[{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Alito","text":"But, if it was a question of federal common law under the pre-Erie regime whether the liability -- the dischargeability of a debt by one partner is discharge -- dischargeable against the other partner, would we not look to what has taken the place of federal common law under Erie, which would be state law, so we would look to state liability law?"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Field v. Mans","cite":"516 U.S. 59","y":1995,"id":"1995/94-967","sc":1,"by":{"0":"32","1":"23, 39","2":"14, 18, 21, 27","3":"7, 11, 18, 23"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"encouraged such falsity by their borrowers for the very purpose of insulating their own claims from discharge.","cite":"516 U.S. at 76"},{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"the law of any particular State.","cite":"516 U.S. at 70"},{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"elements that the common law has defined [fraud] to include.","cite":"516 U.S. at 69"},{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Jackson","text":"I'm relying on Field versus Mans, which suggested and, in fact, I think held that fraud in the Bankruptcy Code is defined by common law principles."},{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Jackson","text":"But Field versus Mans was a bankruptcy case, and we said look at fraud based on common law principles."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Correct, and what Field versus Mans said was for the substantive elements of what is in the statutory text, the word \"fraud,\" that the substantive elements are defined with respect to the common law."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Frank v. Mich. Paper Co.","cite":"179 F. 776","y":1910,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./179/776/","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gilpin v. Merchants’ Bank","cite":"165 F. 607","y":1908,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./165/607/","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gladstone, Realtors v. Village of Bellwood","cite":"441 U.S. 91","y":1979,"id":"1978/77-1493","sc":1,"by":{"0":"33","3":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Grogan v. Garner","cite":"498 U.S. 279","y":1991,"id":"1990/89-1149","sc":1,"by":{"0":"24","1":"26","2":"3, 30","3":"10, 11, 12, 18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"reinforced by the structure of § 523(a),","cite":"498 U.S. 279, 287"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"creditors’ interest in recovering full payment of [certain] debts * * * outweighed the debtors’ interest in a complete fresh start.","cite":"498 U.S. 279, 287"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"was intended to broaden the coverage of the fraud exceptions.","cite":"498 U.S. 279, 290"},{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"a liability defined by state law.","cite":"498 U.S. 279, 283-84"},{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"the issue of nondischargeability [is] a matter of federal law governed by the terms of the Bankruptcy Code.","cite":"498 U.S. at 284"},{"src":"opinion","who":"Opinion of the Court (Justice Barrett)","text":"outweigh[s] the debtors' interest in a complete fresh start,","cite":"498 U. S. 279, 287"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Maybe another way of showing this is by talking about Grogan versus Garner, which is a case where the -- the Court is not dealing with the elements of fraud but what is the standard of proof for fraud."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Hardie v. Swafford Bros. Dry Goods Co.","cite":"165 F. 588","y":1908,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./165/588/","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hartford Underwriters Ins. v. Union Planters Bank, N.A.","cite":"530 U.S. 1","y":2000,"id":"1999/99-409","sc":1,"by":{"0":"32, 44","2":"30","3":"9"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"that the expression of one thing indicates the inclusion of others unless the exclusion is made explicit.","cite":"530 U.S. 1, 8"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"sit to assess the relative merits of different approaches to various bankruptcy problems.","cite":"530 U.S. 1, 13"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"And if you look at the way that Congress is using the word \"the debtor\" or not using \"the debtor,\" it's very similar to what happened in Hartford Underwriters with respect to using the word \"trustee,\" \"only trustee,\" or \"not trustee\" at all."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Husky Int’l Elecs., Inc. v. Ritz","cite":"578 U.S. 356","y":2016,"id":"2015/15-145","sc":1,"by":{"0":"22, 28, 35, 36","1":"15","2":"2, 9, 13, 16","3":"5, 7"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"elements that the common law has defined them to include.","cite":"578 U.S. 356, 360"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"[A]nything that counts as ‘fraud’ and is done with wrongful intent is ‘actual fraud.’","cite":"578 U.S. 356, 360"},{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Sotomayor","text":"So let's look at our decision in Husky. The debtor -- you're saying it has to be the debtor's fraud that counts only. But, in Husky, that case involved fraud by a third party, a shareholder, who fraudulently conveyed assets of the debtor company."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"I -- Husky announced what fraud meant as a matter of common law and said fraudulent conveyance is a form of fraud. Husky did not say just because you have a corporation and an agent and the agent's actions --"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Respectfully, Husky does not expressly say anything with respect to imputation. And the things that it does say with respect to contrasting (a)(2)(A) and other provisions of the code make it very curious that there was -- if there was an imputation holding, it was not discussed."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Jama v. ICE","cite":"543 U.S. 335","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-674","sc":1,"by":{"0":"44","3":"23, 24"}},{"t":"case","label":"James-Dickinson Farm Mortg. Co. v. Harry","cite":"273 U.S. 119","y":1927,"id":"scdb/1926-143","sc":1,"by":{"0":"42","3":"22, 24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"At common law every member of a partnership is subject to … liability","cite":"273 U.S. 119, 123"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Kawaauhau v. Geiger","cite":"523 U.S. 57","y":1998,"id":"1997/97-115","sc":1,"by":{"0":"17","1":"32","3":"3, 18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"aid[ing] the unfortunate debtor by giving him a fresh start in life.","cite":"523 U.S. at 62"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"exceptions to discharge ‘should be confined to those plainly expressed.’","cite":"523 U.S. 57, 62"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Lamar, Archer & Cofrin, LLP v. Appling","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1752","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-1215","sc":1,"by":{"0":"passim","1":"20","2":"10, 18, 20, 27","3":"12"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"broad provisions for the discharge of debts, subject to exceptions.","cite":"138 S. Ct. at 1758"},{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"basic policy … of affording relief only to an ‘honest but unfortunate debtor.’","cite":"138 S. Ct. at 1758"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"it presumptively was aware of the longstanding judicial interpretation ... and intended for it to retain its established meaning.","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1752, 1762"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"‘interpretation of the Bankruptcy Code starts where all such inquiries must begin: with the language of the statute itself.’","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1752, 1759"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Lamie v. U.S. Tr.","cite":"540 U.S. 526","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-693","sc":1,"by":{"0":"43"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"is the existing statutory text, and not predecessor statutes.","cite":"540 U.S. 526, 534"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Marrama v. Citizens Bank of Mass.","cite":"549 U.S. 365","y":2007,"id":"2006/05-996","sc":1,"by":{"0":"16, 43"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"The principal purpose of the Bankruptcy Code is to grant a fresh start to the honest but unfortunate debtor.","cite":"549 U.S. 365, 367"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Marx v. Gen. Revenue Corp.","cite":"568 U.S. 371","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-1175","sc":1,"by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mission Prod. Holdings, Inc. v. Tempnology, LLC","cite":"139 S. Ct. 1652","y":2019,"id":"2018/17-1657","sc":1,"by":{"0":"4, 33","1":"23, 32","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mohamad v. Palestinian Auth.","cite":"566 U.S. 449","y":2012,"id":"2011/11-88","sc":1,"by":{"0":"18","1":"34"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"‘individual’ to denote a natural person, and in particular to distinguish between a natural person and a corporation.’","cite":"566 U.S. 449, 454"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Neal v. Clark","cite":"95 U.S. 704","y":1878,"id":"scdb/1877-139","sc":1,"by":{"0":"16","1":"15, 18, 22, 39","2":"19"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"positive fraud, or fraud in fact, involving moral turpitude or intentional wrong.","cite":"95 U.S. 704, 707-09"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Since Neal versus Clark, even debtors who themselves commit constructive fraud can discharge those debts because they lack fraudulent intent."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"We also have this background rule that is -- you know, runs throughout partnership in all 50 states, through the common law, through both Neal and -- or, excuse me, Strang and its discussion of Neal that partners are liable for the frauds of their partners."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"As for the state law theory, I -- I think I heard the other side suggest that you take whatever state law is to the point where Neal versus Clark might be wrongly decided because you can be liable for fraud without culpable intent under a lot of state laws."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Ragan, Malone & Co. v. Cotton","cite":"200 F. 546","y":1912,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./200/546/","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rodriguez v. FDIC","cite":"140 S. Ct. 713","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-1269","sc":1,"by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sachan v. Huh","cite":"506 B.R. 257","y":2014,"court":"B.A.P. 9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/B.R./506/257/","by":{"0":"11","1":"8","3":"15"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"‘knew or had reason to know’ of [David]’s fraudulent omissions.","cite":"506 B.R. 257, 271-72"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Schwab v. Reilly","cite":"560 U.S. 770","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-538","sc":1,"by":{"0":"17","2":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Strang v. Bradner","cite":"114 U.S. 555","y":1885,"id":"scdb/1884-236","sc":1,"by":{"0":"passim","1":"passim","2":"6, 8, 18, 19, 20","3":"passim"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"refused … to respond to a material question","cite":"114 U.S. at 561"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"basic partnership principles, [‘]if, in the conduct of partnership business, ... one partner makes false or fraudulent misrepresentations ... his partners cannot escape pecuniary responsibility therefor upon the ground that such misrepresentations were made without their knowledge.[’]","cite":"114 U.S. at 561"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"upon the ground that such misrepresentations were made without [her] knowledge.","cite":"114 U.S. at 561"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"is nondischargeable regardless of her knowledge of the fraud.","cite":"114 U.S. 555"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"other than such debts as were by law excepted from the operation of a discharge in bankruptcy.","cite":"114 U.S. at 556"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Nor does Strang, which arose under the repealed 1867 act, control today's code. And while state laws impose vicarious liability for partners' acts, bankruptcy discharge applies different federal law rules to individual debtors because the point of bankruptcy is to eliminate liability."},{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Jackson","text":"And I'm not just relying on Strang. I'm relying on Field versus Mans, which suggested and, in fact, I think held that fraud in the Bankruptcy Code is defined by common law principles."},{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Jackson","text":"-- the whole principle of Strang is gone, the idea that vicarious liability does apply per the common law in this -- this situation?"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Tex. Indus., Inc. v. Radcliff Materials, Inc.","cite":"451 U.S. 630","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-1144","sc":1,"by":{"0":"43","3":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Unicolors, Inc. v. H&M Hennes & Maurtiz, L.P.","cite":"142 S. Ct. 941","y":2022,"id":"2021/20-915","sc":1,"by":{"0":"22","1":"31","3":"15"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[a] knowing misrepresentation … of a material fact.","cite":"142 S. Ct. 941, 949"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Briggs","cite":"141 S. Ct. 467","y":2020,"id":"2020/19-108","sc":1,"by":{"0":"24, 43"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Ron Pair Enters.","cite":"489 U.S. 235","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-1043","sc":1,"by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Wilson","cite":"503 U.S. 329","y":1992,"id":"1991/90-1745","sc":1,"by":{"0":"30, 31"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[t]his silence can make the meaning of a statute somewhat difficult to ascertain.","cite":"503 U.S. at 334"}]},{"t":"case","label":"W.S. Peck Co. v. Lowenbein","cite":"178 F. 178","y":1910,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./178/178/","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Wimberly v. Lab. & Indus. Rels. Comm’n","cite":"479 U.S. 511","y":1987,"id":"1986/85-129","sc":1,"by":{"0":"31"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"the basis for the State’s decision.","cite":"479 U.S. 511, 516"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 101","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/101","subs":["(5)(A)","(12)","(31)","(41)"],"stext":{"head":"§101. Definitions","paras":[[0,"In this title the following definitions shall apply:"],[1,"(1) The term \"accountant\" means accountant authorized under applicable law to practice public accounting, and includes professional accounting association, corporation, or partnership, if so authorized."],[1,"(2) The term \"affiliate\" means—"],[2,"(A) entity that directly or indirectly owns, controls, or holds with power to vote, 20 percent or more of the outstanding voting securities of the debtor, other than an entity that holds such securities—"],[3,"(i) in a fiduciary or agency capacity without sole discretionary power to vote such securities; or"],[3,"(ii) solely to secure a debt, if such entity has not in fact exercised such power to vote;"],[2,"(B) corporation 20 percent or more of whose outstanding voting securities are directly or indirectly owned, controlled, or held with power to vote, by the debtor, or by an entity that directly or indirectly owns, controls, or holds with power to vote, 20 percent or more of the outstanding voting securities of the debtor, other than an entity that holds such securities—"],[3,"(i) in a fiduciary or agency capacity without sole discretionary power to vote such securities; or"],[3,"(ii) solely to secure a debt, if such entity has not in fact exercised such power to vote;"],[2,"(C) person whose business is operated under a lease or operating agreement by a debtor, or person substantially all of whose property is operated under an operating agreement with the debtor; or"],[2,"(D) entity that operates the business or substantially all of the property of the debtor under a lease or operating agreement."],[1,"(3) The term \"assisted person\" means any person whose debts consist primarily of consumer debts and the value of whose nonexempt property is less than $150,000.1"],[1,"(4) The term \"attorney\" means attorney, professional law association, corporation, or partnership, authorized under applicable law to practice law."],[1,"(4A) The term \"bankruptcy assistance\" means any goods or services sold or otherwise provided to an assisted person with the express or implied purpose of providing information, advice, counsel, document preparation, or filing, or attendance at a creditors' meeting or appearing in a case or proceeding on behalf of another or providing legal representation with respect to a case or proceeding under this title."],[1,"(5) The term \"claim\" means—"],[2,"(A) right to payment, whether or not such right is reduced to judgment, liquidated, unliquidated, fixed, contingent, matured, unmatured, disputed, undisputed, legal, equitable, secured, or unsecured; or"],[2,"(B) right to an equitable remedy for breach of performance if such breach gives rise to a right to payment, whether or not such right to an equitable remedy is reduced to judgment, fixed, contingent, matured, unmatured, disputed, undisputed, secured, or unsecured."],[1,"(6) The term \"commodity broker\" means futures commission merchant, foreign futures commission merchant, clearing organization, leverage transaction merchant, or commodity options dealer, as defined in section 761 of this title, with respect to which there is a customer, as defined in section 761 of this title."],[1,"(7) The term \"community claim\" means claim that arose before the commencement of the case concerning the debtor for which property of the kind specified in section 541(a)(2) of this title is liable, whether or not there is any such property at the time of the commencement of the case."],[1,"(7A) The term \"commercial fishing operation\" means—"],[2,"(A) the catching or harvesting of fish, shrimp, lobsters, urchins, seaweed, shellfish, or other aquatic species or products of such species; or"],[2,"(B) for purposes of section 109 and chapter 12, aquaculture activities consisting of raising for market any species or product described in subparagraph (A)."],[1,"(7B) The term \"commercial fishing vessel\" means a vessel used by a family fisherman to carry out a commercial fishing operation."],[1,"(8) The term \"consumer debt\" means debt incurred by an individual primarily for a personal, family, or household purpose."],[1,"(9) The term \"corporation\"—"],[2,"(A) includes—"],[3,"(i) association having a power or privilege that a private corporation, but not an individual or a partnership, possesses;"],[3,"(ii) partnership association organized under a law that makes only the capital subscribed responsible for the debts of such association;"],[3,"(iii) joint-stock company;"],[3,"(iv) unincorporated company or association; or"],[3,"(v) business trust; but"],[2,"(B) does not include limited partnership."],[1,"(10) The term \"creditor\" means—"],[2,"(A) entity that has a claim against the debtor that arose at the time of or before the order for relief concerning the debtor;"],[2,"(B) entity that has a claim against the estate of a kind specified in section 348(d), 502(f), 502(g), 502(h) or 502(i) of this title; or"],[2,"(C) entity that has a community claim."],[1,"(10A) The term \"current monthly income\"—"],[2,"(A) means the average monthly income from all sources that the debtor receives (or in a joint case the debtor and the debtor's spouse receive) without regard to whether such income is taxable income, derived during the 6-month period ending on—"],[3,"(i) the last day of the calendar month immediately preceding the date of the commencement of the case if the debtor files the schedule of current income required by section 521(a)(1)(B)(ii); or"],[3,"(ii) the date on which current income is determined by the court for purposes of this title if the debtor does not file the schedule of current income required by section 521(a)(1)(B)(ii); and"],[2,"(B)(i) includes any amount paid by any entity other than the debtor (or in a joint case the debtor and the debtor's spouse), on a regular basis for the household expenses of the debtor or the debtor's dependents (and in a joint case the debtor's spouse if not otherwise a dependent); and"],[2,"(ii) excludes—"],[3,"(I) benefits received under the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 301 et seq.);"],[3,"(II) payments to victims of war crimes or crimes against humanity on account of their status as victims of such crimes;"],[3,"(III) payments to victims of international terrorism or domestic terrorism, as those terms are defined in section 2331 of title 18, on account of their status as victims of such terrorism; and"],[3,"(IV) any monthly compensation, pension, pay, annuity, or allowance paid under title 10, 37, or 38 in connection with a disability, combat-related injury or disability, or death of a member of the uniformed services, except that any retired pay excluded under this subclause shall include retired pay paid under chapter 61 of title 10 only to the extent that such retired pay exceeds the amount of retired pay to which the debtor would otherwise be entitled if retired under any provision of title 10 other than chapter 61 of that title."],[1,"(11) The term \"custodian\" means—"],[2,"(A) receiver or trustee of any of the property of the debtor, appointed in a case or proceeding not under this title;"],[2,"(B) assignee under a general assignment for the benefit of the debtor's creditors; or"],[2,"(C) trustee, receiver, or agent under applicable law, or under a contract, that is appointed or authorized to take charge of property of the debtor for the purpose of enforcing a lien against such property, or for the purpose of general administration of such property for the benefit of the debtor's creditors."],[1,"(12) The term \"debt\" means liability on a claim."],[1,"(12A) The term \"debt relief agency\" means any person who provides any bankruptcy assistance to an assisted person in return for the payment of money or other valuable consideration, or who is a bankruptcy petition preparer under section 110, but does not include—"],[2,"(A) any person who is an officer, director, employee, or agent of a person who provides such assistance or of the bankruptcy petition preparer;"],[2,"(B) a nonprofit organization that is exempt from taxation under section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986;"],[2,"(C) a creditor of such assisted person, to the extent that the creditor is assisting such assisted person to restructure any debt owed by such assisted person to the creditor;"],[2,"(D) a depository institution (as defined in section 3 of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act) or any Federal credit union or State credit union (as those terms are defined in section 101 of the Federal Credit Union Act), or any affiliate or subsidiary of such depository institution or credit union; or"],[2,"(E) an author, publisher, distributor, or seller of works subject to copyright protection under title 17, when acting in such capacity."],[1,"(13) The term \"debtor\" means person or municipality concerning which a case under this title has been commenced."],[1,"(13A) The term \"debtor's principal residence\"—"],[2,"(A) means a residential structure if used as the principal residence by the debtor, including incidental property, without regard to whether that structure is attached to real property; and"],[2,"(B) includes an individual condominium or cooperative unit, a mobile or manufactured home, or trailer if used as the principal residence by the debtor."],[1,"(14) The term \"disinterested person\" means a person that—"],[2,"(A) is not a creditor, an equity security holder, or an insider;"],[2,"(B) is not and was not, within 2 years before the date of the filing of the petition, a director, officer, or employee of the debtor; and"]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/11/101?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"0":"18, 20, 34","1":"15, 15, 15, 35, 35","2":"1","3":"4"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"person … concerning which a case … has been commenced.","cite":"11 U.S.C. § 101(13)"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3568","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3568","subs":["(1982)"],"by":{"0":"30"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[t]he Attorney General shall give any such person credit","cite":"18 U.S.C. § 3568"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 3304","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/3304","by":{"0":"31"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"no person shall be denied compensation","cite":"26 U.S.C. § 3304(a)(12)"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1254","by":{"0":"2"}},{"t":"usc","label":"42 U.S.C. § 3610","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/3610","subs":["(1976)"],"by":{"0":"33"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 95-598","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/92/2549","by":{"0":"36, 37, 38","1":"22, 34","2":"21","3":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ali v. Fed. Bureau of Prisons","cite":"552 U.S. 214","y":2008,"id":"2007/06-9130","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17","2":"13"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"[R]ead naturally, the word ‘any’ has an expansive meaning, that is, ‘one or some indiscriminately of whatever kind.’","cite":"552 U.S. 214, 219"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Andrus v. Glover Constr. Co.","cite":"446 U.S. 608","y":1980,"id":"1979/79-48","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Badgerow v. Walters","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1310","y":2022,"id":"2021/20-1143","sc":1,"by":{"1":"40, 42"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"‘[w]hen Congress includes particular language in one section of a statute but omits it in another section of the same Act,’ we generally take the choice to be deliberate.","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1310, 1318"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"sound not in text but in policy.","cite":"142 S. Ct. at 1320"}]},{"t":"case","label":"BedRoc Ltd., LLC v. United States","cite":"541 U.S. 176","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-1593","sc":1,"by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bethune-Hill v. Va. State Bd. of Elections","cite":"137 S. Ct. 788","y":2017,"id":"2016/15-680","sc":1,"by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"BFP v. Resol. Tr. Corp.","cite":"511 U.S. 531","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-1370","sc":1,"by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"BP P.L.C. v. Mayor & City Council of Balt.","cite":"141 S. Ct. 1532","y":2021,"id":"2020/19-1189","sc":1,"by":{"1":"43"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"‘even the most formidable’ policy arguments cannot ‘overcome’ a clear statutory directive.","cite":"141 S. Ct. 1532, 1542"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Brown v. Felsen","cite":"442 U.S. 127","y":1979,"id":"1978/78-58","sc":1,"by":{"1":"22","2":"10, 17, 21, 29, 30","3":"11, 17, 18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"In 1903, Congress substituted ‘liabilities’ for ‘judgments.’","cite":"442 U.S. at 138"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"But, under cases like Brown versus Felsen, state law does not define the scope of the federal discharge exceptions. And that's evident from cases like Bullock, where defalcation had a very different meaning under state law, it had no intent requirement, and the Court said, no, bankruptcy discharge is a federal law."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"And that's why cases from Brown versus Felsen onwards have said these discharge exceptions are questions of federal law. You look to federal law rules."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Butner v. United States","cite":"440 U.S. 48","y":1979,"id":"1978/77-1410","sc":1,"by":{"1":"21, 27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chambers v. Kay","cite":"29 Cal. 4th 142","y":2002,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%204th/29/142/","by":{"1":"4"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"shar[e] in the profits and losses","cite":"29 Cal. 4th 142, 151"}]},{"t":"case","label":"City & County of San Francisco v. Sheehan","cite":"575 U.S. 600","y":2015,"id":"2014/13-1412","sc":1,"by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Clark v. Martinez","cite":"543 U.S. 371","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-878","sc":1,"by":{"1":"35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dean v. United States","cite":"556 U.S. 568","y":2009,"id":"2008/08-5274","sc":1,"by":{"1":"36","2":"16"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"focus[] on an event that occurs without respect to a specific actor, and therefore without respect to any actor’s intent or culpability.","cite":"556 U.S. 568, 572"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"[t]he passive voice focuses on an event that occurs without respect to a specific actor, and therefore without respect to any actor’s intent or culpability.","cite":"556 U.S. 568, 572"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"And this Court has recognized in cases like Dean and Wilson, when Congress is using the passive voice without intent requirements, that's when you're more likely to think Congress is indifferent to who's doing something."}]},{"t":"case","label":"(v) Deodati v. M.M. Winkler & Assocs. (In re M.M. Winkler & Assocs.)","cite":"239 F.3d 746","y":2001,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/239/746/","by":{"1":"18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"focuses on the character of the debt, not the culpability of the debtor.","cite":"239 F.3d 746, 749"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Dodd v. United States","cite":"545 U.S. 353","y":2005,"id":"2004/04-5286","sc":1,"by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Encino Motorcars, LLC v. Navarro","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1134","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-1362","sc":1,"by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Food Mktg. Inst. v. Argus Leader Media","cite":"139 S. Ct. 2356","y":2019,"id":"2018/18-481","sc":1,"by":{"1":"33"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"no license to give [statutory] exemption[s] anything but a fair reading.’","cite":"139 S. Ct. 2356, 2366"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Gallardo v. Marstiller","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1751","y":2022,"id":"2021/20-1263","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Garcia v. Vanguard Car Rental USA, Inc.","cite":"540 F.3d 1242","y":2008,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/540/1242/","by":{"1":"24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"shield[s] rental car companies from certain vicarious liability suits","cite":"540 F.3d 1242, 1244"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Gleason v. Seaboard Air Line Ry. Co.","cite":"278 U.S. 349","y":1929,"id":"scdb/1928-040","sc":1,"by":{"1":"27"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"[F]ew doctrines of the law are more firmly established or more in harmony with accepted notions of social policy than that of the liability of the principal without fault of his own.","cite":"278 U.S. 349, 356"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Gleason v. Thaw","cite":"236 U.S. 558","y":1915,"id":"scdb/1914-236","sc":1,"by":{"1":"32, 33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hillman v. Maretta","cite":"569 U.S. 483","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-1221","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17","2":"14"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"[W]here Congress explicitly enumerates certain exceptions to a general prohibition, additional exceptions are not to be implied, in the absence of evidence of a contrary legislative intent.","cite":"569 U.S. 483, 496"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Kloeckner v. Solis","cite":"568 U.S. 41","y":2012,"id":"2012/11-184","sc":1,"by":{"1":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"(vi) Mary M. v. City of Los Angeles","cite":"54 Cal. 3d 202","y":1991,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%203d/54/202/","by":{"1":"28"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"give[s] greater assurance of compensation for the victim.","cite":"54 Cal. 3d 202, 209"}]},{"t":"case","label":"McIntyre v. Kavanaugh","cite":"242 U.S. 138","y":1916,"id":"scdb/1916-031","sc":1,"by":{"1":"20, 25","2":"23","3":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Meyer v. Holley","cite":"537 U.S. 280","y":2003,"id":"2002/01-1120","sc":1,"by":{"1":"passim","3":"20, 21"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"legislates against a legal background of ordinary tort-related vicarious liability rules and consequently intends its legislation to incorporate those rules.","cite":"537 U.S. at 285"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Well, a few responses, and the first of which is, if you look at Meyer versus Holley, which is the main case that Respondent is relying on, the notion that you port in vicarious liability as a matter of common law is not a rule that applies willy-nilly to every federal statute."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Well, because -- because the Bankruptcy Code is not creating a tort, and that rule is specific to creating torts under Meyer versus Holley."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Milavetz, Gallop & Milavetz, P.A. v. United States","cite":"559 U.S. 229","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1119","sc":1,"by":{"1":"20","2":"27","3":"6, 24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"prevent the practice of loading up on debt prior to filing,","cite":"559 U.S. 229, 244"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Miske v. Coxeter","cite":"204 Cal. App. 4th 1249","y":2012,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%20App.%204th/204/1249/","by":{"1":"4, 27, 39"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"the fraud of a copartner acting within the scope of his or her authority.","cite":"204 Cal. App. 4th 1249, 1256"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Morgan v. Sundance","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1708","y":2022,"id":"2021/21-328","sc":1,"by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Murphy v. Smith","cite":"138 S. Ct. 784","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-1067","sc":1,"by":{"1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nat’l Ass’n of Mfrs. v. Dep’t of Def.","cite":"138 S. Ct. 617","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-299","sc":1,"by":{"1":"13, 24"}},{"t":"case","label":"New Orleans, M., & C.R. Co. v. Hanning","cite":"82 U.S. 649","y":1872,"id":"scdb/1872-125","sc":1,"by":{"1":"5"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"The principal is liable for the acts and negligence of the agent in the course of his employment, although he did not authorize or did not know of the acts complained of.","cite":"82 U.S. 649, 657"}]},{"t":"case","label":"OBB Personenverkehr AG v. Sachs","cite":"577 U.S. 27","y":2015,"id":"2015/13-1067","sc":1,"by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Oklahoma v. Castro-Huerta","cite":"142 S. Ct. 2486","y":2022,"id":"2021/21-429","sc":1,"by":{"1":"34"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"the text of a law controls over purported legislative intentions unmoored from any statutory text.","cite":"142 S. Ct. 2486, 2496"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Pa. Dep’t of Pub. Welfare v. Davenport","cite":"495 U.S. 552","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-156","sc":1,"by":{"1":"15, 21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pac. Mut. Life Ins. Co. v. Haslip","cite":"499 U.S. 1","y":1991,"id":"1990/89-1279","sc":1,"by":{"1":"27"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"deters fraud more than a less stringent rule.","cite":"499 U.S. 1, 14"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Puerto Rico v. Franklin Cal. Tax-Free Tr.","cite":"579 U.S. 115","y":2016,"id":"2015/15-233","sc":1,"by":{"1":"23"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"rewrite the statute that Congress has enacted","cite":"579 U.S. 115, 130"}]},{"t":"case","label":"(vii) RadLAX Gateway Hotel, LLC v. Amalgamated Bank","cite":"566 U.S. 639","y":2012,"id":"2011/11-166","sc":1,"by":{"1":"44"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"the pros and cons of [any particular rule] are for the consideration of Congress, not the courts.","cite":"566 U.S. 639, 649"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Siebold v. Berdine","cite":"61 Cal. App. 158","y":1923,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%20App./61/158/","by":{"1":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sullivan v. Glenn","cite":"782 F.3d 378","y":2015,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/782/378/","by":{"1":"29, 30, 31"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"illustrates the limitations of literal interpretation of statutory language.","cite":"782 F.3d at 380"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Tapia v. United States","cite":"564 U.S. 319","y":2011,"id":"2010/10-5400","sc":1,"by":{"1":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Boyle","cite":"469 U.S. 241","y":1985,"id":"1984/83-1266","sc":1,"by":{"1":"41"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"[t]he failure to make a timely filing of a tax return is not excused by the taxpayer’s reliance on an agent.","cite":"469 U.S. 241, 252"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Gonzales","cite":"520 U.S. 1","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-1605","sc":1,"by":{"1":"16","2":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Jones","cite":"565 U.S. 400","y":2012,"id":"2011/10-1259","sc":1,"by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Texas","cite":"507 U.S. 529","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-1729","sc":1,"by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Van Buren v. United States","cite":"141 S. Ct. 1648","y":2021,"id":"2020/19-783","sc":1,"by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Veritex Cmty. Bank v. Osborne (In re Osborne)","cite":"951 F.3d 691","y":2020,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/951/691/","by":{"1":"25, 41","3":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Visa Inc. v. Osborn","cite":"137 S. Ct. 289","y":2016,"id":"2016/15-961","sc":1,"by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Walker v. Citizens State Bank (In re Walker)","cite":"726 F.2d 452","y":1984,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/726/452/","by":{"1":"29, 30, 31","2":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Watson v. United States","cite":"552 U.S. 74","y":2007,"id":"2007/06-571","sc":1,"by":{"1":"36","2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"(viii) Whitfield v. United States","cite":"574 U.S. 265","y":2015,"id":"2014/13-9026","sc":1,"by":{"1":"9, 17"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"no authority to add a limitation the statute plainly does not contain.","cite":"574 U.S. 265, 268-69"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"1 U.S.C. § 1","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/1/1","by":{"1":"12, 35"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 110","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/110","by":{"1":"41"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 365","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/365","by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 523","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/523","subs":["(a)(1)","(a)(2)(A)","(a)(2)(B)","(a)(2)(C)","(a)(3)","(a)(6)","(a)(13)","(a)(14)","(a)(14A)","(a)(14B)","(a)(19)(B)","(a)","(a)(1)(C)","(a)(2)","(a)(2)(B)(iv)","(a)(2)(C)(i)","(a)(2)(C)(i)(I)","(a)(2)(C)(i)(II)","(a)(4)","(a)(9)","(a)(12)","(a)(15)","(a)(18)"],"stext":{"head":"§523. Exceptions to discharge","paras":[[0,"(a) A discharge under section 727, 1141, 1192 1 1228(a), 1228(b), or 1328(b) of this title does not discharge an individual debtor from any debt—"],[1,"(1) for a tax or a customs duty—"],[2,"(A) of the kind and for the periods specified in section 507(a)(3) or 507(a)(8) of this title, whether or not a claim for such tax was filed or allowed;"],[2,"(B) with respect to which a return, or equivalent report or notice, if required—"],[3,"(i) was not filed or given; or"],[3,"(ii) was filed or given after the date on which such return, report, or notice was last due, under applicable law or under any extension, and after two years before the date of the filing of the petition; or"],[2,"(C) with respect to which the debtor made a fraudulent return or willfully attempted in any manner to evade or defeat such tax;"],[1,"(2) for money, property, services, or an extension, renewal, or refinancing of credit, to the extent obtained by—"],[2,"(A) false pretenses, a false representation, or actual fraud, other than a statement respecting the debtor's or an insider's financial condition;"],[2,"(B) use of a statement in writing—"],[3,"(i) that is materially false;"],[3,"(ii) respecting the debtor's or an insider's financial condition;"],[3,"(iii) on which the creditor to whom the debtor is liable for such money, property, services, or credit reasonably relied; and"],[3,"(iv) that the debtor caused to be made or published with intent to deceive; or"],[2,"(C)(i) for purposes of subparagraph (A)—"],[3,"(I) consumer debts owed to a single creditor and aggregating more than $500 2 for luxury goods or services incurred by an individual debtor on or within 90 days before the order for relief under this title are presumed to be nondischargeable; and"],[3,"(II) cash advances aggregating more than $750 2 that are extensions of consumer credit under an open end credit plan obtained by an individual debtor on or within 70 days before the order for relief under this title, are presumed to be nondischargeable; and"],[2,"(ii) for purposes of this subparagraph—"],[3,"(I) the terms \"consumer\", \"credit\", and \"open end credit plan\" have the same meanings as in section 103 of the Truth in Lending Act; and"],[3,"(II) the term \"luxury goods or services\" does not include goods or services reasonably necessary for the support or maintenance of the debtor or a dependent of the debtor;"],[1,"(3) neither listed nor scheduled under section 521(a)(1) of this title, with the name, if known to the debtor, of the creditor to whom such debt is owed, in time to permit—"],[2,"(A) if such debt is not of a kind specified in paragraph (2), (4), or (6) of this subsection, timely filing of a proof of claim, unless such creditor had notice or actual knowledge of the case in time for such timely filing; or"],[2,"(B) if such debt is of a kind specified in paragraph (2), (4), or (6) of this subsection, timely filing of a proof of claim and timely request for a determination of dischargeability of such debt under one of such paragraphs, unless such creditor had notice or actual knowledge of the case in time for such timely filing and request;"],[1,"(4) for fraud or defalcation while acting in a fiduciary capacity, embezzlement, or larceny;"],[1,"(5) for a domestic support obligation;"],[1,"(6) for willful and malicious injury by the debtor to another entity or to the property of another entity;"],[1,"(7) to the extent such debt is for a fine, penalty, or forfeiture payable to and for the benefit of a governmental unit, and is not compensation for actual pecuniary loss, other than a tax penalty—"],[2,"(A) relating to a tax of a kind not specified in paragraph (1) of this subsection; or"],[2,"(B) imposed with respect to a transaction or event that occurred before three years before the date of the filing of the petition;"],[1,"(8) unless excepting such debt from discharge under this paragraph would impose an undue hardship on the debtor and the debtor's dependents, for—"],[2,"(A)(i) an educational benefit overpayment or loan made, insured, or guaranteed by a governmental unit, or made under any program funded in whole or in part by a governmental unit or nonprofit institution; or"],[2,"(ii) an obligation to repay funds received as an educational benefit, scholarship, or stipend; or"],[2,"(B) any other educational loan that is a qualified education loan, as defined in section 221(d)(1) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, incurred by a debtor who is an individual;"],[1,"(9) for death or personal injury caused by the debtor's operation of a motor vehicle, vessel, or aircraft if such operation was unlawful because the debtor was intoxicated from using alcohol, a drug, or another substance;"],[1,"(10) that was or could have been listed or scheduled by the debtor in a prior case concerning the debtor under this title or under the Bankruptcy Act in which the debtor waived discharge, or was denied a discharge under section 727(a)(2), (3), (4), (5), (6), or (7) of this title, or under section 14c(1), (2), (3), (4), (6), or (7) of such Act;"],[1,"(11) provided in any final judgment, unreviewable order, or consent order or decree entered in any court of the United States or of any State, issued by a Federal depository institutions regulatory agency, or contained in any settlement agreement entered into by the debtor, arising from any act of fraud or defalcation while acting in a fiduciary capacity committed with respect to any depository institution or insured credit union;"],[1,"(12) for malicious or reckless failure to fulfill any commitment by the debtor to a Federal depository institutions regulatory agency to maintain the capital of an insured depository institution, except that this paragraph shall not extend any such commitment which would otherwise be terminated due to any act of such agency;"],[1,"(13) for any payment of an order of restitution issued under title 18, United States Code;"],[1,"(14) incurred to pay a tax to the United States that would be nondischargeable pursuant to paragraph (1);"],[1,"(14A) incurred to pay a tax to a governmental unit, other than the United States, that would be nondischargeable under paragraph (1);"],[1,"(14B) incurred to pay fines or penalties imposed under Federal election law;"],[1,"(15) to a spouse, former spouse, or child of the debtor and not of the kind described in paragraph (5) that is incurred by the debtor in the course of a divorce or separation or in connection with a separation agreement, divorce decree or other order of a court of record, or a determination made in accordance with State or territorial law by a governmental unit;"],[1,"(16) for a fee or assessment that becomes due and payable after the order for relief to a membership association with respect to the debtor's interest in a unit that has condominium ownership, in a share of a cooperative corporation, or a lot in a homeowners association, for as long as the debtor or the trustee has a legal, equitable, or possessory ownership interest in such unit, such corporation, or such lot, but nothing in this paragraph shall except from discharge the debt of a debtor for a membership association fee or assessment for a period arising before entry of the order for relief in a pending or subsequent bankruptcy case;"],[1,"(17) for a fee imposed on a prisoner by any court for the filing of a case, motion, complaint, or appeal, or for other costs and expenses assessed with respect to such filing, regardless of an assertion of poverty by the debtor under subsection (b) or (f)(2) of section 1915 of title 28 (or a similar non-Federal law), or the debtor's status as a prisoner, as defined in section 1915(h) of title 28 (or a similar non-Federal law);"],[1,"(18) owed to a pension, profit-sharing, stock bonus, or other plan established under section 401, 403, 408, 408A, 414, 457, or 501(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, under—"],[2,"(A) a loan permitted under section 408(b)(1) of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, or subject to section 72(p) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986; or"],[2,"(B) a loan from a thrift savings plan permitted under subchapter III of chapter 84 of title 5, that satisfies the requirements of section 8433(g) of such title;"],[1,"but nothing in this paragraph may be construed to provide that any loan made under a governmental plan under section 414(d), or a contract or account under section 403(b), of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 constitutes a claim or a debt under this title;"],[1,"(19) that—"],[2,"(A) is for—"],[3,"(i) the violation of any of the Federal securities laws (as that term is defined in section 3(a)(47) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934), any of the State securities laws, or any regulation or order issued under such Federal or State securities laws; or"],[3,"(ii) common law fraud, deceit, or manipulation in connection with the purchase or sale of any security; and"],[2,"(B) results, before, on, or after the date on which the petition was filed, from—"],[3,"(i) any judgment, order, consent order, or decree entered in any Federal or State judicial or administrative proceeding;"],[3,"(ii) any settlement agreement entered into by the debtor; or"],[3,"(iii) any court or administrative order for any damages, fine, penalty, citation, restitutionary payment, disgorgement payment, attorney fee, cost, or other payment owed by the debtor; or"]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/11/523?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"1":"6, 41, passim, passim, 39, 40, 41, 25, 25, 41, 41, 26, 26","2":"3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24, 3, 4, 6, 10, 24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"any debt ... for money ... obtained by ... actual fraud.","cite":"11 U.S.C. 523(a)(2)(A)"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"statement respecting the debtor’s or an insider’s financial condition,","cite":"11 U.S.C. 523(a)(2)(A)"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"does not discharge an individual debtor from any debt * * * for money * * * to the extent obtained by * * * false pretenses, a false representation, or actual fraud.","cite":"11 U.S.C. 523(a)(2)(A)"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"debt * * * for money * * * obtained by * * * false pretenses, a false representation, or actual fraud.","cite":"11 U.S.C. 523(a)(2)(A)"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"Section 523 specifies when the individual debtor, as distinct from others, cannot discharge debts. (a)(2)(A)'s reference to actual fraud targets the debtor's misconduct by requiring fraudulent intent, just as other tort-based exceptions target the debtor's fault."}]},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 727","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/727","subs":["(a)","(a)(2)"],"stext":{"head":"§727. Discharge","paras":[[0,"(a) The court shall grant the debtor a discharge, unless—"],[1,"(1) the debtor is not an individual;"],[1,"(2) the debtor, with intent to hinder, delay, or defraud a creditor or an officer of the estate charged with custody of property under this title, has transferred, removed, destroyed, mutilated, or concealed, or has permitted to be transferred, removed, destroyed, mutilated, or concealed—"],[2,"(A) property of the debtor, within one year before the date of the filing of the petition; or"],[2,"(B) property of the estate, after the date of the filing of the petition;"],[1,"(3) the debtor has concealed, destroyed, mutilated, falsified, or failed to keep or preserve any recorded information, including books, documents, records, and papers, from which the debtor's financial condition or business transactions might be ascertained, unless such act or failure to act was justified under all of the circumstances of the case;"],[1,"(4) the debtor knowingly and fraudulently, in or in connection with the case—"],[2,"(A) made a false oath or account;"],[2,"(B) presented or used a false claim;"],[2,"(C) gave, offered, received, or attempted to obtain money, property, or advantage, or a promise of money, property, or advantage, for acting or forbearing to act; or"],[2,"(D) withheld from an officer of the estate entitled to possession under this title, any recorded information, including books, documents, records, and papers, relating to the debtor's property or financial affairs;"],[1,"(5) the debtor has failed to explain satisfactorily, before determination of denial of discharge under this paragraph, any loss of assets or deficiency of assets to meet the debtor's liabilities;"],[1,"(6) the debtor has refused, in the case—"],[2,"(A) to obey any lawful order of the court, other than an order to respond to a material question or to testify;"],[2,"(B) on the ground of privilege against self-incrimination, to respond to a material question approved by the court or to testify, after the debtor has been granted immunity with respect to the matter concerning which such privilege was invoked; or"],[2,"(C) on a ground other than the properly invoked privilege against self-incrimination, to respond to a material question approved by the court or to testify;"],[1,"(7) the debtor has committed any act specified in paragraph (2), (3), (4), (5), or (6) of this subsection, on or within one year before the date of the filing of the petition, or during the case, in connection with another case, under this title or under the Bankruptcy Act, concerning an insider;"],[1,"(8) the debtor has been granted a discharge under this section, under section 1141 of this title, or under section 14, 371, or 476 of the Bankruptcy Act, in a case commenced within 8 years before the date of the filing of the petition;"],[1,"(9) the debtor has been granted a discharge under section 1228 or 1328 of this title, or under section 660 or 661 of the Bankruptcy Act, in a case commenced within six years before the date of the filing of the petition, unless payments under the plan in such case totaled at least—"],[2,"(A) 100 percent of the allowed unsecured claims in such case; or"],[2,"(B)(i) 70 percent of such claims; and"],[2,"(ii) the plan was proposed by the debtor in good faith, and was the debtor's best effort;"],[1,"(10) the court approves a written waiver of discharge executed by the debtor after the order for relief under this chapter;"],[1,"(11) after filing the petition, the debtor failed to complete an instructional course concerning personal financial management described in section 111, except that this paragraph shall not apply with respect to a debtor who is a person described in section 109(h)(4) or who resides in a district for which the United States trustee (or the bankruptcy administrator, if any) determines that the approved instructional courses are not adequate to service the additional individuals who would otherwise be required to complete such instructional courses under this section (The United States trustee (or the bankruptcy administrator, if any) who makes a determination described in this paragraph shall review such determination not later than 1 year after the date of such determination, and not less frequently than annually thereafter.); or"],[1,"(12) the court after notice and a hearing held not more than 10 days before the date of the entry of the order granting the discharge finds that there is reasonable cause to believe that—"],[2,"(A) section 522(q)(1) may be applicable to the debtor; and"],[2,"(B) there is pending any proceeding in which the debtor may be found guilty of a felony of the kind described in section 522(q)(1)(A) or liable for a debt of the kind described in section 522(q)(1)(B)."],[0,"(b) Except as provided in section 523 of this title, a discharge under subsection (a) of this section discharges the debtor from all debts that arose before the date of the order for relief under this chapter, and any liability on a claim that is determined under section 502 of this title as if such claim had arisen before the commencement of the case, whether or not a proof of claim based on any such debt or liability is filed under section 501 of this title, and whether or not a claim based on any such debt or liability is allowed under section 502 of this title."],[0,"(c)(1) The trustee, a creditor, or the United States trustee may object to the granting of a discharge under subsection (a) of this section."],[0,"(2) On request of a party in interest, the court may order the trustee to examine the acts and conduct of the debtor to determine whether a ground exists for denial of discharge."],[0,"(d) On request of the trustee, a creditor, or the United States trustee, and after notice and a hearing, the court shall revoke a discharge granted under subsection (a) of this section if—"],[1,"(1) such discharge was obtained through the fraud of the debtor, and the requesting party did not know of such fraud until after the granting of such discharge;"],[1,"(2) the debtor acquired property that is property of the estate, or became entitled to acquire property that would be property of the estate, and knowingly and fraudulently failed to report the acquisition of or entitlement to such property, or to deliver or surrender such property to the trustee;"],[1,"(3) the debtor committed an act specified in subsection (a)(6) of this section; or"],[1,"(4) the debtor has failed to explain satisfactorily—"],[2,"(A) a material misstatement in an audit referred to in section 586(f) of title 28; or"],[2,"(B) a failure to make available for inspection all necessary accounts, papers, documents, financial records, files, and all other papers, things, or property belonging to the debtor that are requested for an audit referred to in section 586(f) of title 28."],[0,"(e) The trustee, a creditor, or the United States trustee may request a revocation of a discharge—"],[1,"(1) under subsection (d)(1) of this section within one year after such discharge is granted; or"],[1,"(2) under subsection (d)(2) or (d)(3) of this section before the later of—"],[2,"(A) one year after the granting of such discharge; and"],[2,"(B) the date the case is closed."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/11/727?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"1":"6","2":"3, 24, 3, 24, 3, 24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"with intent to hinder, delay, or defraud a creditor or an officer of the estate charged with custody of property.","cite":"11 U.S.C. 727(a)(2)"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 1141","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/1141","subs":["(d)(6)(A)","(d)(2)","(d)(6)"],"stext":{"head":"§1141. Effect of confirmation","paras":[[0,"(a) Except as provided in subsections (d)(2) and (d)(3) of this section, the provisions of a confirmed plan bind the debtor, any entity issuing securities under the plan, any entity acquiring property under the plan, and any creditor, equity security holder, or general partner in the debtor, whether or not the claim or interest of such creditor, equity security holder, or general partner is impaired under the plan and whether or not such creditor, equity security holder, or general partner has accepted the plan."],[0,"(b) Except as otherwise provided in the plan or the order confirming the plan, the confirmation of a plan vests all of the property of the estate in the debtor."],[0,"(c) Except as provided in subsections (d)(2) and (d)(3) of this section and except as otherwise provided in the plan or in the order confirming the plan, after confirmation of a plan, the property dealt with by the plan is free and clear of all claims and interests of creditors, equity security holders, and of general partners in the debtor."],[0,"(d)(1) Except as otherwise provided in this subsection, in the plan, or in the order confirming the plan, the confirmation of a plan—"],[1,"(A) discharges the debtor from any debt that arose before the date of such confirmation, and any debt of a kind specified in section 502(g), 502(h), or 502(i) of this title, whether or not—"],[2,"(i) a proof of the claim based on such debt is filed or deemed filed under section 501 of this title;"],[2,"(ii) such claim is allowed under section 502 of this title; or"],[2,"(iii) the holder of such claim has accepted the plan; and"],[1,"(B) terminates all rights and interests of equity security holders and general partners provided for by the plan."],[0,"(2) A discharge under this chapter does not discharge a debtor who is an individual from any debt excepted from discharge under section 523 of this title."],[0,"(3) The confirmation of a plan does not discharge a debtor if—"],[1,"(A) the plan provides for the liquidation of all or substantially all of the property of the estate;"],[1,"(B) the debtor does not engage in business after consummation of the plan; and"],[1,"(C) the debtor would be denied a discharge under section 727(a) of this title if the case were a case under chapter 7 of this title."],[0,"(4) The court may approve a written waiver of discharge executed by the debtor after the order for relief under this chapter."],[0,"(5) In a case in which the debtor is an individual—"],[1,"(A) unless after notice and a hearing the court orders otherwise for cause, confirmation of the plan does not discharge any debt provided for in the plan until the court grants a discharge on completion of all payments under the plan;"],[1,"(B) at any time after the confirmation of the plan, and after notice and a hearing, the court may grant a discharge to the debtor who has not completed payments under the plan if—"],[2,"(i) the value, as of the effective date of the plan, of property actually distributed under the plan on account of each allowed unsecured claim is not less than the amount that would have been paid on such claim if the estate of the debtor had been liquidated under chapter 7 on such date;"],[2,"(ii) modification of the plan under section 1127 is not practicable; and"],[2,"(iii) subparagraph (C) permits the court to grant a discharge; and"],[1,"(C) the court may grant a discharge if, after notice and a hearing held not more than 10 days before the date of the entry of the order granting the discharge, the court finds that there is no reasonable cause to believe that—"],[2,"(i) section 522(q)(1) may be applicable to the debtor; and"],[2,"(ii) there is pending any proceeding in which the debtor may be found guilty of a felony of the kind described in section 522(q)(1)(A) or liable for a debt of the kind described in section 522(q)(1)(B);"],[1,"and if the requirements of subparagraph (A) or (B) are met."],[1,"(6) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the confirmation of a plan does not discharge a debtor that is a corporation from any debt—"],[2,"(A) of a kind specified in paragraph (2)(A) or (2)(B) of section 523(a) that is owed to a domestic governmental unit, or owed to a person as the result of an action filed under subchapter III of chapter 37 of title 31 or any similar State statute; or"],[2,"(B) for a tax or customs duty with respect to which the debtor—"],[3,"(i) made a fraudulent return; or"],[3,"(ii) willfully attempted in any manner to evade or to defeat such tax or such customs duty."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/11/1141?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"1":"14, 35","2":"24, 24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"a debtor that is a corporation.","cite":"11 U.S.C. 1141(d)(6)"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 1305","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/1305","subs":["(c)"],"by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 924","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/924","subs":["(d)(1)"],"by":{"1":"36"}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 30106","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/30106","stext":{"head":"§30106. Rented or leased motor vehicle safety and responsibility","paras":[[0,"(a) In General.—An owner of a motor vehicle that rents or leases the vehicle to a person (or an affiliate of the owner) shall not be liable under the law of any State or political subdivision thereof, by reason of being the owner of the vehicle (or an affiliate of the owner), for harm to persons or property that results or arises out of the use, operation, or possession of the vehicle during the period of the rental or lease, if—"],[1,"(1) the owner (or an affiliate of the owner) is engaged in the trade or business of renting or leasing motor vehicles; and"],[1,"(2) there is no negligence or criminal wrongdoing on the part of the owner (or an affiliate of the owner)."],[0,"(b) Financial Responsibility Laws.—Nothing in this section supersedes the law of any State or political subdivision thereof—"],[1,"(1) imposing financial responsibility or insurance standards on the owner of a motor vehicle for the privilege of registering and operating a motor vehicle; or"],[1,"(2) imposing liability on business entities engaged in the trade or business of renting or leasing motor vehicles for failure to meet the financial responsibility or liability insurance requirements under State law."],[0,"(c) Applicability and Effective Date.—Notwithstanding any other provision of law, this section shall apply with respect to any action commenced on or after the date of enactment of this section without regard to whether the harm that is the subject of the action, or the conduct that caused the harm, occurred before such date of enactment."],[0,"(d) Definitions.—In this section, the following definitions apply:"],[1,"(1) Affiliate.—The term \"affiliate\" means a person other than the owner that directly or indirectly controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with the owner. In the preceding sentence, the term \"control\" means the power to direct the management and policies of a person whether through ownership of voting securities or otherwise."],[1,"(2) Owner.—The term \"owner\" means a person who is—"],[2,"(A) a record or beneficial owner, holder of title, lessor, or lessee of a motor vehicle;"],[2,"(B) entitled to the use and possession of a motor vehicle subject to a security interest in another person; or"],[2,"(C) a lessor, lessee, or a bailee of a motor vehicle, in the trade or business of renting or leasing motor vehicles, having the use or possession thereof, under a lease, bailment, or otherwise."],[1,"(3) Person.—The term \"person\" means any individual, corporation, company, limited liability company, trust, association, firm, partnership, society, joint stock company, or any other entity."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/49/30106?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"1":"24","3":"21"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 99-554","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/100/3117","by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Archer v. Warner","cite":"538 U.S. 314","y":2003,"id":"2002/01-1418","sc":1,"by":{"2":"17","3":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hersh v. United States ex rel. Mukasey","cite":"553 F.3d 743","y":2008,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/553/743/","by":{"2":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Johnson v. United States","cite":"559 U.S. 133","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-6925","sc":1,"by":{"2":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Raleigh v. Illinois Dep’t of Revenue","cite":"530 U.S. 15","y":2000,"id":"1999/99-387","sc":1,"by":{"2":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ransom v. FIA Card Servs., N.A.","cite":"562 U.S. 61","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-907","sc":1,"by":{"2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Russello v. United States","cite":"464 U.S. 16","y":1983,"id":"1983/82-472","sc":1,"by":{"2":"15, 26"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"includes particular language in one section of a statute but omits it in another section of the same Act.","cite":"464 U.S. at 23"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Travelers Cas. & Sur. Co. of Am. v. Pacific Gas & Elec. Co.","cite":"549 U.S. 443","y":2007,"id":"2006/05-1429","sc":1,"by":{"2":"11","3":"18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"long recognized that the basic federal rule in bankruptcy is that state law governs the substance of claims.","cite":"549 U.S. 443, 450"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Sotelo","cite":"436 U.S. 268","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-1800","sc":1,"by":{"2":"31"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"prevents a bankrupt from getting an entirely ‘fresh start,’","cite":"436 U.S. 268, 279-280"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"provide[] little assistance in construing a section expressly designed to make some debts nondischargeable.","cite":"436 U.S. at 280"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 301","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/301","by":{"2":"1"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 307","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/307","by":{"2":"1"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 501","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/501","by":{"2":"29"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 521","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/521","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"2":"29"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 701","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/701","by":{"2":"3, 4, 6, 10, 24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 1101","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/1101","by":{"2":"3, 24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 1201","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/1201","by":{"2":"3, 24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 1301","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/1301","by":{"2":"3, 24"}},{"t":"law","label":"National Bankruptcy Review Commission Act, Pub. L. No. 103-394","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/108/4147","by":{"2":"22"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 116-54","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/116/public/54","by":{"2":"2"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 581-589a","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/581-589a","by":{"2":"1"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bruesewitz v. Wyeth LLC","cite":"562 U.S. 223","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-152","sc":1,"by":{"3":"24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"[p]ost-enactment legislative history … is not a legitimate tool of statutory interpretation.","cite":"562 U.S. 223, 242"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Cummings v. HPG Int’l","cite":"244 F.3d 16","y":2001,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/244/16/","by":{"3":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dinsmore-Poff v. Alvord","cite":"972 P.2d 978","y":1999,"court":"Alaska","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P.2d/972/978/","by":{"3":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Egbert v. Boule","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1793","y":2022,"id":"2021/21-147","sc":1,"by":{"3":"15"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"not limited to the precise arguments [she] made below.","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1793, 1806 n.3"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Gebser v. Lago Vista Indep. Sch. Dist.","cite":"524 U.S. 274","y":1998,"id":"1997/96-1866","sc":1,"by":{"3":"20"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"‘agents,’ and so does not expressly call for application of agency principles.","cite":"524 U.S. 274, 283"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"It is confined to when Congress is creating torts. And it's honestly honored more in the breach sometimes if you look at cases like Gebser --"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"If it were the case that whenever you mention the word \"fraud\" you would have vicarious liability in any statute, Gebser or other cases mentioning common law torts would have come out the other way."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Harris","text":"No, the direct liability would be the line I think the Court drew in Gebser and is the traditional -- I think the traditional concept in the Restatement, Second, of Torts, which is, when you say the debtor or another actor in a statute, who do you mean for purposes of direct liability if the statute does not include vicarious liability?"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Jesner v. Arab Bank, PLC","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1386","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-499","sc":1,"by":{"3":"20"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"[C]ourts must exercise ‘great caution’ before recognizing new forms of liability","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1386, 1403"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Mary Pickford Co. v. Bayly Bros.","cite":"86 P.2d 102","y":1939,"court":"Cal.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P.2d/86/102/","by":{"3":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Monell v. Dep’t of Soc. Serv.","cite":"436 U.S. 658","y":1978,"id":"1977/75-1914","sc":1,"by":{"3":"21"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"cannot be easily read to impose","cite":"436 U.S. 658, 692"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Peter v. Nantkwest, Inc.","cite":"140 S. Ct. 365","y":2019,"id":"2019/18-801","sc":1,"by":{"3":"17"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":3,"text":"reach [something] it would not otherwise include.","cite":"140 S. Ct. 365, 372-73"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Siegel v. Fitzgerald","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1770","y":2022,"id":"2021/21-441","sc":1,"by":{"3":"19"}},{"t":"usc","label":"42 U.S.C. § 1983","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1983","by":{"3":"21"}},{"t":"reg","label":"26 C.F.R. § 1.6061-1","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-26/section-1.6061-1","by":{"3":"9"}}]};